From: Mlte0907 <Mlte0907@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 02:44:10 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/6] feat(preset): restore autonomous preset on alpha.1 with
 pangu gate
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

- 从本地 6 提交栈恢复 autonomous preset（目录扫描自动发现，无需其他代码）
- 保留两轮 Pangu 强化（pangu protocol + BLOCKING GATE + PER-TURN REMINDER）
- 裁除 plan-mode 工具声明与文字引用（@deepseek-ai/dsh-plan-mode 在 alpha.1 不存在）
---
 .../presets/autonomous/agent.cordis.yml       | 343 ++++++++++++++++++
 .../presets/autonomous/preset.yml             |   3 +
 2 files changed, 346 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 packages/preset/agent-presets/presets/autonomous/agent.cordis.yml
 create mode 100644 packages/preset/agent-presets/presets/autonomous/preset.yml

diff --git a/packages/preset/agent-presets/presets/autonomous/agent.cordis.yml b/packages/preset/agent-presets/presets/autonomous/agent.cordis.yml
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..6343223bf6
--- /dev/null
+++ b/packages/preset/agent-presets/presets/autonomous/agent.cordis.yml
@@ -0,0 +1,343 @@
+# The `autonomous` agent preset: the standard coding agent tuned for unattended
+# operation, modeled on ZCode's Agent mode.
+#
+# The toolset matches `standard` — shell, filesystem, search, background jobs,
+# skills, goals, plan mode, compaction, delegation, web — because the tuning of
+# this preset is DOCTRINAL, not mechanical: the persona below carries the
+# operating rules of an agent left to work on its own (act on reversible steps,
+# keep a todo list current, delegate breadth, verify before claiming done).
+# The rows that differ from `standard` back that doctrine up:
+#
+#   * `tool-todo` forbids parallel in-progress items — the list is a plan with
+#     one front, not a batch queue.
+#   * `tool-subagent` omits `modelSelectionSettings`, so children inherit the
+#     parent's route instead of sampling the per-session user setting — the
+#     delegation story of this mode is "same engine, separate context".
+#   * two dedicated spawn rows give the doctrine its delegation shapes:
+#     `explore` (read-only fan-out searches, its own persona plus a
+#     `toolFilter` removing `edit`/`write`) and `judge` (the same read-only
+#     scoping, but the child reads rendered images itself with `read_image`
+#     and returns an evidence-backed verdict per image).
+#   * `tool-plan-mode` sits inside the planning group: the model can propose
+#     the switch into plan mode for non-trivial work, while the exit review
+#     stays with the user.
+#   * a `pangu-memory` row mounts the Pangu MCP memory server when the
+#     deployment opts in through the environment — the long-term-memory
+#     doctrine in the persona keeps its promise only when the server is there.
+#   * the workflow-engine and Ralph-loop rows of `standard` are absent: this
+#     mode's loop is the conversation itself, and no row here publishes a
+#     service, so the delegation rows sit flat with no `isolate` realm.
+#
+# This file is an AGENT-PLANE composition. The roster mounts it ONCE under a
+# standing scope; every session naming it joins by scope parentage, so the
+# tools and prompt sections registered here cover each joined agent while a
+# session's own state stays keyed per Session/Agent inside the plugins. The
+# host composition (`base.cordis.yml` + `web.cordis.yml`) keeps everything a
+# preset must not own: the registries themselves, the sandbox and approval
+# stack, persistence, and the model route.
+#
+# A service row here MUST sit inside a group carrying an `isolate` realm —
+# without one it publishes into the root realm, where another preset
+# publishing the same name collides and `dsh-agent-presets` rejects the mount.
+# Rows that only register into host registries (every row below) need none.
+
+# ── identity ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
+
+# The preset's own persona, shadowing the deployment default for this agent.
+# `{{model}}` and `{{cwd}}` resolve from the agent's own route and workspace.
+# The doctrine here is the preset: it tells the model HOW to run unattended;
+# the rows below only make the promise keepable.
+- id: persona
+  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-persona'
+  config:
+    text: >-
+      You are an autonomous coding agent powered by the {{model}} model, running on the DeepSeek Harness. Your working directory is {{cwd}}.
+
+
+      ## ⚠️ BLOCKING GATE: Pangu memory (MUST execute before ANY tool call)
+
+
+      DO NOT proceed until you have called `mcp__pangu__pangu_search_memories`. This is not optional. This is not a suggestion. This is the FIRST action of EVERY session. Even if the user asks "hello", search first. Even if the task seems trivial, search first. The search takes 2 seconds; skipping it costs cross-session learning forever.
+
+
+      **Step 1 — BEFORE your first tool call:** Call `mcp__pangu__pangu_search_memories` with a query derived from the user's message. Report relevant findings in 1-2 sentences, then proceed.
+
+
+      **Step 2 — AFTER completing meaningful work:** Call `mcp__pangu__pangu_add_memory` to capture: what was learned, what broke and why, what to do differently next time. Skip trivial one-off actions.
+
+
+      **Step 3 — BEFORE ending the session:** Check if anything worth remembering was learned; if so, write it.
+
+
+      When `mcp__pangu__` tools are not available, skip silently.
+
+
+      **🔍 PER-TURN REMINDER:** Every time you receive a user message, before responding, check: "Have I called pangu_search_memories for this message?" If not, do it NOW. If yes, proceed. This check costs nothing; forgetting it costs everything.
+
+
+      ## Operating rules
+
+
+      Operating stance. You are operating autonomously: the user is not watching in real time and cannot answer questions mid-task. For reversible actions that follow from the request, proceed without asking; stop and ask only for destructive actions or genuine scope changes the user must decide. When you have enough information to act, act — do not re-derive what is already established, and do not narrate options you will not pursue.
+
+
+      Work in steps the user can see. For any multi-step task, write the plan with todo_write and keep it current: exactly one item in_progress at a time, marked completed the moment it is done, dropped when it stops mattering. The list is the working plan, not a report written afterwards.
+
+
+      Delegate breadth, keep depth. When a task means sweeping many files, directories, or naming conventions and you only need the conclusion, delegate to an `explore` subagent instead of reading everything yourself; write its prompt self-contained — the goal, what to return, the breadth — because it starts with no context from this conversation. Launch independent delegations in the same response so they run concurrently, and do not repeat the same search yourself while waiting. Work that needs your own judgment — reviewing or auditing specific code, multi-step changes — stays with you. When work produces or depends on rendered visuals — screenshots, mockups, before/after images — delegate the acceptance pass to a `judge` subagent with the image paths and the acceptance criteria: it reads the images itself and returns a pass/fail verdict with evidence for each. Never grade your own visual output; hand it to the judge. A subagent's final message is shown only to you: relay what matters in your own words.
+
+
+      Verify before you claim done. Run the checks, tests, or builds that cover the change and read what they say. Report outcomes faithfully: failures with their output, skipped steps as skipped. Prefer durable evidence over a passing glance; state plainly what is done and verified.
+
+
+      Communicate results, not process. Open the final answer with the outcome — what happened or what you found — then supporting detail in complete sentences that read on their own. Brief progress notes between tool calls are fine; everything the user needs must be in the final message, because that is the only one they are guaranteed to read. Match the reply to the question: a simple question gets a direct answer in prose, not headers and tables.
+
+
+      ask_user_question is for choices that are genuinely the user's to make — trade-offs with no conventional default, or destructive actions. Where code lives, how current behavior works, and which obvious convention applies are yours to resolve by inspection.
+
+- id: agent-instructions
+  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-instructions'
+  config:
+    maxBytes: 65536
+
+# ── shell ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
+
+# `shell-env` stays in the HOST composition: `apps/cli/src/web.ts` injects it to
+# publish `DSH_WEB_URL`/`DSH_WEB_MODE`, and a host row that injects a service is
+# the criterion for host-plane ownership — injection resolves before any session
+# exists, so there is no agent to key by. Behind a preset realm those variables
+# never reached the model's shell at all. Both shell tools consume the host
+# registry from here; their executors (`bash-sandbox`/`pwsh-sandbox`) are
+# host-plane too.
+- id: tool-bash
+  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-bash'
+  disabled: !!js process.platform === 'win32'
+
+- id: tool-pwsh
+  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-pwsh'
+  disabled: !!js process.platform !== 'win32'
+
+# ── filesystem ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
+
+# Both register into the host `tools` registry and provide nothing, so
+# they need no realm. The `fs` service and its policy stay in the host.
+- id: tool-fs
+  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-fs'
+
+- id: tool-fs-search
+  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-fs-search'
+  config:
+    sampleOverCapGlobResults: false
+
+# ── background jobs ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
+
+# Only the model-facing controls. The task REGISTRY stays on the host plane:
+# its producers sit outside any realm this file could put it in — `tool-bash`
+# above resolves it with `ctx.get`, and an entry-local realm here is invisible
+# to every sibling row, so `run_in_background` would answer "background jobs
+# unavailable" while these controls sat in the catalog. The registry is keyed by
+# owning agent anyway, so one host instance serves every session. What a preset
+# chooses is whether its agent can collect and stop background work at all —
+# unattended work leans on it, so this preset keeps it.
+- id: tool-jobs
+  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-jobs'
+
+# ── skills ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
+
+# The skill REGISTRY lives in the host composition and is layered per scope:
+# these rows register into THIS preset's layer of it, so they need no realm.
+# `skill-filesystem` contributes local-root discovery for agents on this preset, and
+# `tool-skill` gives them the catalog and loader; the merged catalog also
+# carries whatever the deployment registered globally (repository plugins).
+- id: skill-filesystem
+  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-skill-filesystem'
+
+- id: tool-skill
+  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-skill'
+
+# ── goals ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
+
+# The goal service and session driver stay on the host plane, where the Gateway
+# can resolve them. The human command and model-facing tool register into this
+# preset's scoped layers.
+- id: command-goal
+  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-command-goal'
+
+- id: tool-goal
+  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-goal'
+
+# ── compaction ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
+
+# `compaction-basic` reads `toolResultPrune` through `ctx.get`, so the pruner must
+# share this realm rather than sit outside it.
+#
+# `tokenMeter` is deliberately NOT in this realm: the meter stays on the HOST
+# plane, and the rows here resolve that one instance. It keys every fold by
+# Session, and owns the context-meter projection units the browser reads for
+# every session — behind a realm those units would come and go with whichever
+# presets happen to be mounted. What a preset chooses is whether its agent
+# compacts at all, which is `compaction-basic` below. Unattended sessions run
+# long, so compaction is load-bearing here, not optional.
+- id: compaction
+  name: cordis:group
+  group: true
+  isolate:
+    compaction: true
+    toolResultPruner: true
+  config:
+    - id: compaction-basic
+      name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-compaction-basic'
+
+    - id: command-compact
+      name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-command-compact'
+
+    - id: tool-result-pruner
+      name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-compaction-tool-result-pruner'
+      config:
+        thresholdChars: 8192
+        headChars: 4096
+        tailChars: 1024
+
+# ── delegation ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
+
+# The `subagents` registry and its spawn/fork backends live in the HOST
+# composition: the registry is a process singleton whose cross-session queries
+# the api-proxy serves to the browser, and a provider name may only be
+# registered once. These rows contribute the delegation TOOLS, which resolve
+# that host registry — they publish no service, so unlike `standard`'s
+# delegation group (which isolates the workflow engine) they sit flat, needing
+# no realm.
+#
+# Delegation doctrine lives in the persona above; these rows are its
+# mechanical form. All default to `maxDepth: 3`.
+#
+# `subagent` — the general delegation. `modelSelectionSettings` is deliberately
+# omitted: children inherit the parent's route rather than sampling the
+# `subagent-model-selection` user setting, keeping the "same engine, separate
+# context" story (and keeping the inherited history eligible for prompt-cache
+# reuse on the child). `continuable` keeps a finished child's conversation
+# addressable through `send_message`.
+- id: tool-subagent-control
+  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-subagent-control'
+
+- id: tool-subagent-list-agents
+  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-subagent-control/list-agents'
+
+- id: tool-subagent
+  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-subagent'
+  config:
+    provider: spawn
+    toolName: subagent
+    backgroundMode: continuable
+
+# `subagent_fork` — same-model continuation of THIS conversation with the
+# inherited history intact, so the child's prefix stays eligible for KV Cache
+# reuse. Model selection is omitted so provider/model stay equal to the parent;
+# fork is continuable like `subagent`.
+- id: tool-subagent-fork
+  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-subagent'
+  config:
+    provider: fork
+    toolName: subagent_fork
+    backgroundMode: continuable
+
+# `explore` — the read-only fan-out row. `persona` shadows the deployment
+# persona for every child this row spawns, and `toolFilter` removes the
+# mutation tools from the child's catalog AND execution — the search-side
+# guarantee the persona's prose alone cannot make. Tool names must exist in the
+# child's catalog (`tool-fs` above registers both) or the mount fails loud.
+# One-shot by default: an exploration returns its report and is done.
+- id: tool-subagent-explore
+  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-subagent'
+  config:
+    provider: spawn
+    toolName: explore
+    backgroundMode: one-shot
+    persona: >-
+      You are a read-only exploration agent. Search, read, and reason; never
+      modify anything: edit and write are removed from you, and bash must stay
+      read-only — no file writes or redirects into files, no commits, no state
+      changes. Ground every claim in what you actually read and cite it as
+      path:line. Answer the prompt you were given and nothing besides; your
+      final message is the only thing returned to the caller, so it must carry
+      the conclusion, the evidence, and enough references to act on — the
+      caller will not re-run your searches.
+    toolFilter:
+      deny:
+        - edit
+        - write
+
+# `judge` — the visual acceptance row. Same read-only scoping as `explore`,
+# but the persona makes the child READ the images itself (`read_image` is part
+# of `tool-fs` above) and return one evidence-backed verdict per image, so the
+# model never grades its own visual output. `read_image` refuses to execute on
+# a text-only route: judging needs an image-capable child model.
+- id: tool-subagent-judge
+  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-subagent'
+  config:
+    provider: spawn
+    toolName: judge
+    backgroundMode: one-shot
+    persona: >-
+      You are a visual acceptance judge. You receive image paths and the
+      acceptance criteria; read EVERY image yourself with read_image before
+      judging anything — a screenshot you have not opened is not evidence. For
+      each image return one verdict line: pass or fail, then the issues you
+      can actually see, each tied to what is visible in that image; a clean
+      programmatic check quoted at you is not a verdict. Judge only what is
+      visible; do not speculate about code or causes. Read-only: edit and
+      write are removed from you, and bash must stay read-only. Your final
+      message is the only thing returned to the caller — the verdicts and the
+      evidence are the whole deliverable.
+    toolFilter:
+      deny:
+        - edit
+        - write
+
+# ── long-term memory (Pangu, opt-in) ────────────────────────────────────────
+
+# The memory doctrine in the persona keeps its promise only when the server is
+# there, so the row is opt-in by environment instead of a shipped assumption:
+# it activates only when `PANGU_API_KEY` is exported (the server authenticates
+# requests), and `PANGU_MCP_URL` moves the endpoint off the local default.
+# Pangu publishes 400+ tools; the allow list carries the four the doctrine
+# names, because every registered tool is catalog tokens the model pays for on
+# every request. `mcp-client` injects only `tools`, so the row needs no realm;
+# `failOnStartupError` defaults to false, so a server that is down degrades to
+# absent tools rather than a session that cannot start.
+- id: pangu-memory
+  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-mcp-client'
+  disabled: !!js (!process.env.PANGU_API_KEY)
+  config:
+    serverName: pangu
+    transport: streamable-http
+    url: !!js (process.env.PANGU_MCP_URL || 'http://127.0.0.1:19529/mcp')
+    headers:
+      X-API-Key: !!js (process.env.PANGU_API_KEY || '')
+    tools:
+      allow:
+        - pangu_search_memories
+        - pangu_fts_search
+        - pangu_recall
+        - pangu_add_memory
+
+# ── remaining model-facing rows ─────────────────────────────────────────────
+
+- id: tool-ask-user
+  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-ask-user'
+
+# `allowParallelInProgress: false` is the todo discipline of this mode: the
+# list mirrors ZCode's working plan — exactly one front at a time — instead of
+# a batch queue. `standard` relaxes this to `true`.
+- id: tool-todo
+  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-todo'
+  config:
+    allowParallelInProgress: false
+
+# The `web` service and its search provider stay in the host composition; only
+# the model-facing tool is per-session.
+- id: tool-web
+  name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-web'
+  config:
+    fetch: true
+    searchTimeoutMs: 60000
diff --git a/packages/preset/agent-presets/presets/autonomous/preset.yml b/packages/preset/agent-presets/presets/autonomous/preset.yml
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..385648971d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/packages/preset/agent-presets/presets/autonomous/preset.yml
@@ -0,0 +1,3 @@
+name: 自主模式
+description: 仿照 ZCode Agent 模式的自主编码 Agent：先探索后动手，可逆步骤自主推进，用任务清单跟踪进度，把只读探索分派给子代理，并在宣称完成前先验证。
+order: 5
-- 
